{
  "dom_id": "step-e6190",
  "name": "Example: Factoring a Constant Out of the Norm",
  "problem": "If $\\left\\Vert {- \\dfrac{ \\mathbf{x} }{2} } \\right\\Vert = 5,$ find $\\Vert 3 \\mathbf{x} \\Vert.$",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that we can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\Vert k \\mathbf{x} \\Vert= \\vert k \\vert \\cdot \\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n$$\n\nUsing this fact, we can solve for the norm $\\Vert x \\Vert$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016-\\frac{x}{2}\u2016 & =5 \\\\ \u2016-\\frac{1}{2}x\u2016 & =5 \\\\ |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\cdot \u2016x\u2016 & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \u2016x\u2016 & =5 \\\\ \u2016x\u2016 & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $\\Vert 3 \\mathbf{x} \\Vert \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u20163x\u2016 & =3\\cdot \u2016x\u2016 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
